  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure overhead will allow for radiational cooling \ntonight. Radiational cooling conditions are already occuring, \nwith most places reporting calm and clear skies in many places. \nTemperatures this evening are already running a few degrees \nbelow the forecast, so I have continued to lower low \ntemperatures with these trends. This morning, expect to wake up \nto temperatures in the teens north of the mountains and mid-20s \nelsewhere.\n\nStarting on Sunday, an upper-level trough exits the \nnortheastern US and is replaced by a broad ridge. In general, \nupper-level ridging should allow for temperatures to be more \nmild through much of next week and temperatures do look to run a\nlittle above average on Sunday and Monday.\n\nA nor'easter well offshore could usher enough moisture into the\narea Monday night that a fog bank may build over the coastal \nplain. If fog develops, it could linger at least through the \nlate morning on Tuesday. The nor'easter itself will stay south \nand will not bring meaningful winds nor precipitation to \nnorthern New England.\n\nHowever, high pressure to the north especially towards the \nmiddle of next week may lead to more seasonable temperatures \nthen. No precipitation is forecast next week.",
